        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Complete Privacy: A 100% Client-Side Private List Sorter
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Whether you are alphabetizing employee rosters, reordering confidential client portfolios, or organizing proprietary API endpoints, list sorting frequently involves private information. Many cloud-based text utilities transmit your data across the internet to remote servers, exposing confidential records to network interception, third-party analytics, and database caching.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          FastToolVerse operates under a zero-compromise client-side architecture: <strong>all sorting operations, Unicode collations, deduplication routines, and randomizations execute strictly inside your browser's local sandbox memory</strong>. Your text is never uploaded to any remote server, no cookies track your data, and no logs are retained. You can disconnect from Wi-Fi after opening the page, and the tool will continue functioning with zero degradation in speed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- H2 2: Collation & Natural Sort -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          The Science of String Collation: Natural Sorting vs ASCII Binary Comparison
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          In basic computer programming, sorting is performed by comparing the Unicode character codes (ASCII values) of consecutive characters. This produces notorious sorting anomalies when handling numbers:
        </p>
        <div class="not-prose my-4 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 font-mono text-xs text-purple-300 overflow-x-auto space-y-1">
          <div class="text-rose-400">// Naive ASCII Lexicographical Sort:</div>
          <div>["Item 1", "Item 10", "Item 11", "Item 2", "Item 20", "Item 3"]</div>
          <div class="pt-2 text-emerald-400">// FastToolVerse Natural Collation Sort:</div>
          <div>["Item 1", "Item 2", "Item 3", "Item 10", "Item 11", "Item 20"]</div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          FastToolVerse uses modern ECMAScript Internationalization (<code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">Intl.Collator</code>) with <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">&#123; numeric: true &#125;</code>. This parses numerical tokens within strings as true multi-digit integers, ensuring natural, intuitive human ordering across version numbers, chapter headings, and numbered files.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- H2 3: Fisher-Yates Randomization -->
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Unbiased Randomization: The Fisher-Yates Shuffle Algorithm
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          A common anti-pattern in amateur web randomizers is using <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1 py-0.5 rounded text-purple-600 dark:text-purple-400">array.sort(() =&gt; Math.random() - 0.5)</code>. Mathematically, this produces severe statistical bias because not all permutations have equal probability.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          FastToolVerse implements the gold-standard <strong>Fisher-Yates (Knuth) Shuffle</strong>. Starting from the end of the array, the algorithm swaps each item with a randomly chosen preceding item. This guarantees a mathematically uniform probability distribution of $O(N)$ linear complexity, making it trustworthy for fair raffles, classroom teams, and unbiased experimental sampling.
        </p>
